Every writer has a purpose when organising a story. Some writers want to create suspense, while others want readers to understand a character's emotions before learning what caused them.

Changing the order of events or controlling the speed of the story can completely change how readers experience the narrative.

A well-organised narrative helps readers:

understand events clearly;
stay interested;
feel suspense or excitement;
connect with characters;
remember important moments.

Good organisation keeps readers engaged from the first sentence to the last.

A flashback takes the reader back to something that happened before the main events of the story.

Instead of continuing forward in time, the writer pauses the present action to reveal an earlier event.

Flashbacks often explain:

why a character behaves in a certain way;
an important relationship;
a past mistake;
a hidden secret.

Flashbacks help writers:

provide background information;
explain characters' actions;
create emotional connections;
answer questions readers may have.

Without a flashback, readers might not fully understand the importance of present events.

Foreshadowing helps writers:

build suspense;
encourage readers to make predictions;
create tension;
make later events feel believable.

Readers enjoy looking back and recognising the clues that were hidden throughout the story.

A cyclical structure begins and ends in a similar way.

The ending often returns to:

the same place;
the same event;
the same idea;
or even the same sentence.

This gives readers a feeling of completeness.

It can:

create a satisfying ending;
highlight how characters have changed;
reinforce the story's message;
remind readers of important themes.

Fast pacing often appears during:

action scenes;
chases;
arguments;
exciting discoveries.

Short sentences and quick events make readers feel excited.

Slow pacing is often used when writers want readers to:

notice details;
understand emotions;
build suspense;
appreciate important moments.

Longer descriptions slow the story down.

Sequencing is the order in which a writer presents events.

Events do not always have to be told in the exact order they happened.

A writer may:

begin near the end;
include flashbacks;
tell two stories at the same time;
reveal important information later.

Good sequencing keeps readers interested while ensuring the story still makes sense.

A story should feel connected from beginning to end.

This is called cohesion.

Cohesion helps readers move naturally from one event to the next.

Writers create cohesion by:

referring back to earlier events;
repeating important ideas;
maintaining consistent characters;
linking paragraphs smoothly.

Without cohesion, a story can feel confusing or disconnected.

Paragraphs organise ideas into clear sections.

A new paragraph is often started when:

a new character begins speaking;
the setting changes;
time changes;
a new idea begins;
the action shifts.

Well-planned paragraphs make stories easier to read and understand.

Large blocks of text can confuse readers, while effective paragraphing improves clarity.

Readers often remember how a story made them feel.

That feeling is created by the writer's organisational choices.

These features help writers:

create mystery;
build suspense;
reveal information at the right time;
control emotions;
guide readers through the story.

Strong writers do not organise events by accident. Every structural choice has a purpose.

