Many writers are tempted to begin writing as soon as they have an idea. However, experienced writers know that planning is one of the most important stages of the writing process.

Planning helps writers:

organise ideas logically;
avoid leaving out important details;
maintain a clear structure;
create effective beginnings and endings;
keep readers interested throughout the story.

A few minutes spent planning can greatly improve the quality of a narrative.

A writer should always think about the intended audience.

Different audiences enjoy different types of stories.

For example:

Young Children

Stories often:
follow a simple linear structure;
use clear language;
have happy endings.

Teenagers

Stories may include:
greater suspense;
more complex characters;
unexpected twists;
flashbacks and foreshadowing.

Adults

Stories may:
explore deeper themes;
use complex structures;
encourage readers to think carefully about events.

Understanding the audience helps writers make suitable structural choices.

Writers also think about their purpose.

To Entertain

The writer may use:
exciting action;
suspense;
cliff-hangers;
surprising endings.

To Inspire

The writer may:
show a character overcoming challenges;
end with hope;
focus on personal growth.

To Teach a Lesson

The writer may:
build events towards an important message;
show the consequences of characters' decisions.

The purpose of the narrative influences every structural decision the writer makes.

