A writer's culture often influences:

the setting;
the characters;
the traditions;
the language;
the examples used.

Imagine two stories about sharing food.

One story may describe a family gathering around a traditional South African braai.

Another may describe a family celebrating a festival in another country.

The settings are different because of culture, but both stories may explore the same theme of family and togetherness.

Understanding culture helps readers appreciate why writers make different choices.

The time period in which a text is written also affects the writer's ideas and language.

Older texts may:

use more formal language;
describe life without modern technology;
focus on challenges that were important in the past.

Modern texts may:

include mobile phones and social media;
use more informal language;
describe present-day issues.

Although these texts are different, they may still explore similar themes such as friendship, honesty or determination.

When comparing texts, look for similarities such as:

the same theme;
similar character qualities;
similar messages;
similar emotions.

Example

Both Lindiwe and Oliver:

value education;
show determination;
overcome challenges;
remain positive.

These similarities help readers understand the writers' shared message.

Comparing texts helps readers:

understand different cultures;
appreciate different viewpoints;
recognise common human experiences;
think critically;
support opinions with evidence.

It also reminds us that people around the world often share similar hopes, fears and dreams, even when they live very different lives.

