Vocabulary contributes to voice, but voice is not simply about giving characters different words.

A convincing voice can be shaped through:

what the character notices;
what they ignore;
their opinions;
sentence patterns;
humour;
confidence or uncertainty;
internal thoughts;
dialogue;
reactions to other people;
repeated habits of expression.

Suppose two characters see a dog running towards them.

Voice A
That dog was enormous. Not large. Enormous. The sort of animal that could probably swallow one of my shoes without noticing.

Voice B
The dog was perhaps thirty kilograms, broad through the shoulders and moving quickly. It did not appear aggressive.

The first narrator exaggerates and reacts emotionally.

The second observes carefully and precisely.

The writer has created difference through the way each mind processes the same information.

Sustaining a voice does not mean a character can never change.

Characters may become:

more confident;
frightened;
angry;
hopeful;
uncertain;
mature.

But the development should feel connected to the person already established.

For example:

Beginning
There was absolutely no reason to be nervous. It was just a competition.

Later
I kept telling myself there was no reason to be nervous. My hands, unfortunately, had stopped listening.

The narrator's confidence has weakened, but the dry humour remains.

That helps the voice stay consistent.

Dialogue is another powerful way to establish distinctive voice.

Imagine three characters reacting to the same instruction:

“We have to cross the river.”

Character A
“Fine. Where's the bridge?”

Character B
“You said river. You did not say anything about crossing it.”

Character C
“Excellent. I've always wanted to find out whether these boots are actually waterproof.”

Each response suggests something different about the speaker.

Effective dialogue should therefore do more than pass information between characters.

It can reveal:

personality;
relationships;
confidence;
humour;
fear;
disagreement;
attitude.

Writing from multiple viewpoints can allow you to:

show different interpretations;
reveal information one character lacks;
create misunderstandings;
develop several characters;
increase dramatic irony;
challenge the reader's first judgement;
show how the same event affects people differently.

However, using several narrators does not automatically make a story sophisticated.

Each viewpoint must contribute something.

If two narrators simply repeat exactly the same information, the second perspective may feel unnecessary.

Readers should never have to guess randomly whose perspective they are following.

You can signal changes through:

headings;
chapter breaks;
paragraph or section breaks;
names;
clear changes of location;
a strongly distinctive voice.

For example:

LEO
By eight o'clock, I had accepted that Maya was not coming.

MAYA
At eight o'clock, I was still trapped inside the lift.

This change is immediate and clear.

If viewpoint changes occur without signalling, the writing may become confusing.

